{
  "title": "My throat hurts",
  "title_target": "Боли ме гърлото",
  "goal": "Describe aches and symptoms at the pharmacy and the doctor's. The pattern flips again — it isn't you who hurts, but **the body part that hurts you**.",
  "bekal": [
    "using **боли ме** and **болят ме** correctly",
    "naming the body parts people complain about most",
    "saying since when something has been happening with **от**",
    "reading the instructions for medicine",
    "asking for emergency help"
  ],
  "dialogs": [
    {
      "title": "Диалог — At the pharmacy",
      "intro": "Ani falls ill in her first winter. Notice the pattern *боли ме* — the body part is the subject, and the verb agrees with that body part.",
      "lines": [
        {
          "speaker": "Аптекарка:",
          "text": "[Добър|Do-BĂR] [ден|den]. [Какво|Kak-VO] [ви|vee] [е|eh]?",
          "id": "Good afternoon. What seems to be the problem?",
          "literal": "good — day. what — to-you — is?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ани:",
          "text": "[Боли|Bo-LEE] [ме|meh] [гърлото|GĂR-lo-to] [и|ee] [имам|EE-mam] [температура|tem-peh-ra-TOO-ra].",
          "id": "My throat hurts and I have a fever.",
          "literal": "hurts — me — throat-the — and — I-have — temperature."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Аптекарка:",
          "text": "[От|Ot] [кога|ko-GA]?",
          "id": "Since when?",
          "literal": "from — when?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ани:",
          "text": "[От|Ot] [вчера|VCHEH-ra]. [Не|Neh] [мога|MO-ga] [да|da] [спя|spya].",
          "id": "Since yesterday. I can't sleep.",
          "literal": "from — yesterday. not — I-can — that — I-sleep."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Аптекарка:",
          "text": "[Боли|Bo-LEE] [ли|lee] [ви|vee] [главата|gla-VA-ta]?",
          "id": "Does your head hurt too?",
          "literal": "hurts — (question) — you — head-the?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ани:",
          "text": "[Малко|MAL-ko]. [И|Ee] [ме|meh] [болят|bo-LYAT] [очите|O-chee-teh].",
          "id": "A little. And my eyes hurt.",
          "literal": "a-little. and — me — they-hurt — eyes-the."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Аптекарка:",
          "text": "[Имате|EE-ma-teh] [ли|lee] [алергия|a-ler-GEE-ya] [към|kăm] [лекарства|leh-KARST-va]?",
          "id": "Are you allergic to any medicine?",
          "literal": "you-have — (question) — allergy — to — medicines?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ани:",
          "text": "[Не|Neh], [нямам|NYA-mam].",
          "id": "No, I'm not.",
          "literal": "no, I-don't-have."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Аптекарка:",
          "text": "[Вземайте|VZEH-mai-teh] [тези|TEH-zee] [хапчета|HAP-cheh-ta] — [три|tree] [пъти|PĂ-tee] [на|na] [ден|den], [след|sled] [ядене|YA-deh-neh].",
          "id": "Take these tablets — three times a day, after meals.",
          "literal": "take — these — tablets — three — times — in — day, after — eating."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ани:",
          "text": "[А|A] [за|za] [гърлото|GĂR-lo-to]?",
          "id": "And for the throat?",
          "literal": "and — for — throat-the?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Аптекарка:",
          "text": "[Този|TO-zee] [спрей|sprey]. [И|Ee] [пийте|PEEY-teh] [топъл|TO-păl] [чай|chai] [с|s] [мед|med].",
          "id": "This spray. And drink warm tea with honey.",
          "literal": "this — spray. and — drink — warm — tea — with — honey."
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Ани:",
          "text": "[Благодаря|Bla-go-da-RYA]. [Колко|KOL-ko] [струва|STROO-va] [всичко|FSEECH-ko]?",
          "id": "Thank you. How much is it altogether?",
          "literal": "thank-you. how-much — costs — everything?"
        },
        {
          "speaker": "Аптекарка:",
          "text": "[Дванайсет|Dva-NAI-set] [лева|LEH-va]. [Оздравявайте|Oz-dra-VYA-vai-teh]!",
          "id": "Twelve leva. Get well soon!",
          "literal": "twelve — leva. get-well!"
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        "⚠️ **Боли ме flips the sentence like харесва ми.** Literally it means \"it hurts me\", and the subject is **the body part**. So the verb agrees with the number of body parts: *Боли ме главата* (one head) but **Болят** *ме очите* (two eyes), *краката* (two legs), *зъбите* (many teeth).",
        "⚠️ **The person word here takes the object form, not the receiver form.** It's *боли **ме***, not *боли ми*. Compare: *харесва **ми*** (to me) but *боли **ме*** (me). This is the one place where the two sets of little words get mixed up, so learn the set: **ме, те, го, я, ни, ви, ги**.",
        "**The body part is always definite.** *Боли ме главата*, not *боли ме глава*. It makes sense: the head in question is obviously your own, so it is already specific — English says \"my head\", Bulgarian says \"the head\".",
        "**Какво ви е? is the most common way to ask what's wrong** and literally means \"what is with you?\". The casual form is *Какво ти е?* The same question is used when someone looks down — not just for physical illness.",
        "**От кога? means \"since when?\"** and the answer uses *от* too: *от вчера, от два дни, от една седмица*. It's the same preposition used for where you're from in Unit 2 — Bulgarian uses it for any starting point.",
        "**Вземайте, not вземете.** The first form marks a repeated action — taking medicine three times a day for several days. The second marks a single time. The same difference runs through all Bulgarian verbs, and you will learn it as a system at level A2.",
        "**Оздравявайте! — \"get well soon\" — is said to someone who is ill.** To a friend: *Оздравявай!* It's also what people say if you sneeze twice; after the first sneeze it's usually *Наздраве!*",
        "**The emergency number in Bulgaria is 112**, as everywhere in the EU, and operators speak English. Sentences to memorize right now: **Помощ!** (*POMOSHT* — help!) and **Извикайте линейка!** (*eez-vee-KAI-teh lee-NEY-ka* — call an ambulance!)."
      ],
      "grammars": [
        {
          "title": "Боли ме — the pattern for aches and pains",
          "body": [
            "The verb has only two forms you need: **боли** for one body part, **болят** for more than one."
          ],
          "table": {
            "head": [
              "Bulgarian",
              "Say it",
              "Literally",
              "English"
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "Боли ме главата.",
                "bo-LEE meh gla-VA-ta",
                "hurts me head-the",
                "I have a headache."
              ],
              [
                "Боли ме гърлото.",
                "bo-LEE meh GĂR-lo-to",
                "hurts me throat-the",
                "My throat hurts."
              ],
              [
                "Болят ме очите.",
                "bo-LYAT meh O-chee-teh",
                "hurt(pl) me eyes-the",
                "My eyes hurt."
              ],
              [
                "Боли ли те коремът?",
                "bo-LEE lee teh ko-REH-măt",
                "hurts (question) you stomach-the",
                "Does your stomach hurt?"
              ],
              [
                "Не ме боли нищо.",
                "neh meh bo-LEE NEESH-to",
                "not me hurts nothing",
                "Nothing hurts."
              ],
              [
                "Вчера ме болеше главата.",
                "VCHEH-ra meh bo-LEH-sheh gla-VA-ta",
                "yesterday me was-hurting head-the",
                "Yesterday I had a headache."
              ]
            ]
          },
          "after": [
            "The last row uses the past form **болеше**, one of the few imperfect forms worth knowing at A1, because aches are almost always about an ongoing state, not a sudden event.",
            "If you don't need to name the body part, just say: *Боли ме.* (It hurts.)"
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Body parts",
          "body": [
