        {
          "type": "kotak",
          "title": "Течение — the draft all of Bulgaria fears",
          "items": [
            "If you open two windows at once on a Bulgarian bus, train or classroom, someone will quickly close one, saying **течение!** (*teh-CHEH-nee-eh*) — a cross-draft.",
            "The belief is sincere: air flowing through a room is thought to cause headaches, facial nerve inflammation and bad colds — even on a hot day. It's no joke, and there's no point arguing.",
            "English speakers may find the idea less strange than it seems: \"catching a chill\" and \"catching a cold\" come from the same thinking. And the Bulgarian word for a cold, **настинка**, comes from a root meaning \"to get cold\" — exactly the same logic."
          ]
        }
